MEMORY AND PROCESS INVESTIGATION (MCP SAFE)

Pico W RP2040 architecture: kernel executes from Flash XIP;
user programs are RAM-resident position-independent images.
Six 24 KiB task slots, 4 KiB reserved stack each. Kernel and
network buffers also use SRAM. Do not treat /dev/mem as a RAM file.

START READ-ONLY:
  ps                        active processes and PIDs
  mem -s                    list symbol tables
  mem -s kernel             kernel symbols
  mem -s sh                 shell symbol offsets
  hexdump /dev/shm 0 64     bounded shared-memory bytes
  hexdump /dev/mem 0x20000000 32
  cat /proc/net/memory      network memory diagnostics

'mem' without -s is interactive/full-screen; never use it with
MCP execute_command. 'watch' is likewise interactive. Symbol
information: /proc/sym/kernel shows kernel symbol addresses;
user-app symbol tables are slot-relative; do not interpret
those offsets as absolute addresses without a load base.

hexdump usage:
  hexdump [file [offset [count]]]
  /dev/mem requires explicit offset and count.
  Offsets accept decimal and 0x-prefixed values.
  For MCP: prefer 16, 32 or 64 bytes. 512 raw bytes may print
  several KiB of text, stressing the 4096-byte response limit.
  Large reads may expose secrets/uninitialized data.

Investigation sequence:
  1. ps -> identify process and slot occupancy.
  2. mem -s -> discover symbols, not guessed offsets.
  3. hexdump /dev/mem ADDRESS 32 -> read just target bytes.
  4. Compare with /proc/sym/kernel or user symbol offset.
  5. Use trace to correlate memory behavior with syscalls.

Writes to arbitrary /dev/mem are dangerous and may crash the OS.
Never attempt via MCP without explicit authorization and a safe
verified region. Don't probe unmapped addresses blindly.
